    <section id="s1">
      <h2>1. What is Disk Forensics?</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>
      <p>
        <span class="highlight">Disk forensics</span> is the branch of digital forensics dedicated to acquiring,
        preserving, analyzing, and presenting data found on storage media — hard drives, SSDs, USB drives, memory cards,
        and optical discs — in a manner that is both <strong>scientifically sound</strong> and <strong>legally
          admissible</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Think of a crime scene investigator dusting for fingerprints. A disk forensics examiner does the same, but
        instead of fingerprints, they look for digital artifacts: deleted files, hidden partitions, timestamps, browser
        history, metadata, and encrypted volumes.
      </p>

      <div class="analogy">
        <strong>🔍 Analogy:</strong> Imagine your hard drive as a massive filing cabinet in a library. Even after you
        throw a document in the trash, it might still sit in the rubbish bin, or the librarian's index card for it might
        still exist. Disk forensics is about finding those abandoned index cards and reconstructing shredded documents.
      </div>

      <h3>Core Objectives</h3>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🔒</div>
          <h4>Preservation</h4>
          <p>Capture an exact, unaltered copy of storage media without modifying any bit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🔎</div>
          <h4>Analysis</h4>
          <p>Examine files, metadata, timestamps, and artifacts to reconstruct events.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">📋</div>
          <h4>Documentation</h4>
          <p>Maintain a detailed, auditable record of every action taken.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">⚖️</div>
          <h4>Presentation</h4>
          <p>Present findings in court in a clear, unambiguous, reproducible way.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Where Is Disk Forensics Used?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Law Enforcement:</strong> Investigating cybercrime, fraud, child exploitation, and homicide.</li>
        <li><strong>Corporate Investigations:</strong> Employee misconduct, data theft, intellectual property leaks.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Incident Response:</strong> Identifying how attackers entered a network and what they did.</li>
        <li><strong>Civil Litigation:</strong> Gathering evidence in lawsuits involving digital records.</li>
        <li><strong>Data Recovery:</strong> Recovering accidentally deleted or corrupted files for legitimate use.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

