      <p>
        Disk forensics traces its roots to the <strong>1980s</strong> when law enforcement began encountering computers
        as crime tools. The FBI established the first computer analysis laboratory around 1984. Early investigators used
        primitive tools — basically <code>dd</code> (disk duplicator) commands on Unix systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>1990s</strong> saw the establishment of the International Organization on Computer Evidence (IOCE)
        in 1993, bringing standardization. Commercial tools like <strong>EnCase</strong> (1998) revolutionized the field
        by offering GUI-based imaging and analysis. The 2000s brought open-source tools like <strong>The Sleuth
          Kit</strong> and <strong>Autopsy</strong>, democratizing forensic analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today, disk forensics must contend with <span class="highlight">terabyte-scale drives</span>, solid-state
        storage with TRIM commands, full-disk encryption (BitLocker, FileVault), and cloud storage that may hold
        critical artifacts not found on the device itself.
      </p>
    </section>

