    <section id="s3">
      <h2>3. Disk Imaging — The Foundation of Forensics</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>

      <p>
        <span class="highlight">Disk imaging</span> is the process of creating a bit-for-bit, sector-by-sector copy of a
        storage medium. It is the single most critical step in any forensic investigation because it allows examiners to
        work on a copy, leaving the original evidence untouched.
      </p>

      <div class="callout info">
        <div class="callout-title">🔑 Golden Rule</div>
        Never work on the original evidence. Always image first, analyze the image. Every investigation starts with an
        exact clone.
      </div>

      <h3>Why Not Just Copy Files?</h3>
      <p>
        A simple file copy using Windows Explorer or Finder <strong>misses everything</strong> that makes forensics
        valuable:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Deleted files still occupying sectors not yet overwritten</li>
        <li>File slack space (unused space at the end of file clusters)</li>
        <li>Unallocated space between partitions</li>
        <li>Boot sectors, partition tables, and MBR data</li>
        <li>Bad sectors that may contain hidden data</li>
        <li>Metadata (MAC times: Modified, Accessed, Created)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A forensic image captures <strong>every single bit</strong> — even empty space — giving you a complete snapshot
        of the drive's state at a precise moment in time.</p>

      <h3>The Write Blocker — The Examiner's Shield</h3>
      <p>
        Before connecting a drive for imaging, forensic examiners use a <strong>write blocker</strong> — either hardware
        or software — that physically prevents any write operations from reaching the source drive. Without it, even
        plugging a drive into Windows can alter timestamps, update partition tables, or create new files (like thumbnail
        caches).
      </p>

