      <h3>Hash Verification — Proving Integrity</h3>
      <p>
        After imaging, a <strong>cryptographic hash</strong> (MD5 or SHA-256) is calculated for both the original drive
        and the image. If the hashes match, it proves the image is an exact, unmodified copy — bit for bit. This hash
        value becomes critical evidence in court.
      </p>

      <pre># Linux dd imaging command
dd if=/dev/sdb of=/mnt/evidence/disk_image.dd bs=4096 conv=noerror,sync

# Generate MD5 hash
md5sum /mnt/evidence/disk_image.dd &gt; disk_image.md5

# Verify against original (via hardware write blocker)
md5sum /dev/sdb | diff - disk_image.md5
    </pre>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Critical Note</div>
        Never run <code>dd</code> without a write blocker on the source drive. A single accidental write invalidates the
        evidence and could make it inadmissible in court.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
    <section id="s4">
      <h2>4. Types of Disk Images</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>
      <p>Different forensic scenarios call for different image formats. Each has trade-offs between size, features, and
        tool compatibility.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Extension</th>
            <th>Key Features</th>
            <th>Best For</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Raw/DD</strong></td>
            <td><code>.dd / .img / .raw</code></td>
            <td>No compression, bit-for-bit copy, universally compatible</td>
            <td>Simple cases, maximum compatibility</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>EnCase EWF</strong></td>
            <td><code>.E01</code></td>
            <td>Compression, metadata, case info, hash built-in, segmented files</td>
            <td>Law enforcement, commercial labs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>AFF</strong></td>
            <td><code>.aff / .afd</code></td>
            <td>Open standard, compression, encryption, annotations</td>
            <td>Academic/open-source environments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>AFF4</strong></td>
            <td><code>.aff4</code></td>
            <td>Modern, faster, multi-threading, better compression</td>
            <td>Large-scale enterprise investigations</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>VMDK/VHD</strong></td>
            <td><code>.vmdk / .vhd</code></td>
            <td>Mountable virtual disk, used in VMs</td>
            <td>Cloud and virtual machine forensics</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Sparse vs. Full Images</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Full Image:</strong> Captures every sector including empty space. Largest size but most complete.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Sparse Image:</strong> Only captures allocated (used) sectors. Faster and smaller but misses
          unallocated space data.</li>
        <li><strong>Logical Image:</strong> Captures only files and folders, not raw sectors. Fastest but least
          forensically complete.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 5 -->
    <section id="s5">
      <h2>5. File Systems Explained</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>

      <p>
        A <span class="highlight">file system</span> is the organizational structure an operating system uses to store,
        retrieve, and manage data on a storage device. Understanding file systems is fundamental to disk forensics
        because different file systems store metadata differently, leave different artifacts, and recover differently.
      </p>

      <div class="analogy">
        <strong>📚 Analogy:</strong> A file system is like the Dewey Decimal System in a library. Without it, books
        (data) would be scattered randomly. The file system provides the index so the OS knows: "File X is in sectors
        1042–1087."
      </div>

      <h3>How Storage Is Organized</h3>

