      <h3>Major File Systems in Detail</h3>

      <h4>NTFS — New Technology File System (Windows)</h4>
      <p>
        The default file system for modern Windows. Forensically rich because it stores <strong>everything in the Master
          File Table (MFT)</strong> — a database where each file has an entry including timestamps (4 timestamps:
        Created, Modified, Access, MFT Changed), permissions, and data streams.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>MFT:</strong> Master record of every file and folder</li>
        <li><strong>Alternate Data Streams (ADS):</strong> Hidden data attached to a file — malware often hides here
        </li>
        <li><strong>$Recycle.Bin:</strong> Stores deleted files with original path and deletion timestamp</li>
        <li><strong>Journal Logs:</strong> <code>$LogFile</code> and <code>$UsnJrnl</code> record file system changes
        </li>
        <li><strong>Volume Shadow Copies:</strong> Previous versions of files — a goldmine for forensics</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>FAT/FAT32/exFAT — File Allocation Table (USB, SD, Legacy)</h4>
      <p>
        Older, simpler file system used on USB drives, SD cards, and older Windows systems. Uses a <strong>File
          Allocation Table</strong> — a linked list mapping clusters to files. When a file is deleted, only the first
        character of its directory entry is overwritten with <code>0xE5</code> — the actual data remains recoverable.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>FAT12:</strong> Floppy disks (obsolete)</li>
        <li><strong>FAT16:</strong> DOS/early Windows (partitions up to 2GB)</li>
        <li><strong>FAT32:</strong> USB/SD up to 2TB, max file size 4GB</li>
        <li><strong>exFAT:</strong> Modern flash drives, supports large files</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>ext2/ext3/ext4 — Linux File Systems</h4>
      <p>
        Linux's primary file systems. Use an <strong>inode</strong> structure — each file has an inode storing metadata
        (permissions, timestamps, pointers to data blocks). When a file is deleted, the inode is marked free but data
        often persists.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>ext2:</strong> No journaling (slower recovery after crash)</li>
        <li><strong>ext3:</strong> Added journaling (tracks changes before committing)</li>
        <li><strong>ext4:</strong> Current standard — larger volumes, extents, better performance</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>APFS — Apple File System (macOS/iOS)</h4>
      <p>
        Apple's modern file system optimized for flash storage. Features built-in encryption, snapshots, and space
        sharing. Forensically challenging because it uses <strong>copy-on-write</strong> semantics and timestamps with
        nanosecond precision. The <code>.journal</code> and snapshot data are critical forensic artifacts.
      </p>

      <h4>HFS+ — macOS Legacy</h4>
      <p>
        Apple's older file system, still found on older Macs and external drives. Stores file metadata in the
        <strong>Catalog File</strong> (a B-tree). Deleted files remain in the catalog until space is reused.
      </p>

