    <!-- SECTION 6 -->
    <section id="s6">
      <h2>6. How Files Are Stored, Modified &amp; Deleted</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>

      <h3>File Storage — The Life of a File</h3>
      <p>
        When you save a file, the OS performs several steps:
      </p>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Allocation</h4>
            <p>The file system finds free clusters on the disk and marks them as allocated in the allocation table (FAT)
              or MFT (NTFS).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Data Write</h4>
            <p>Raw file data is written to those clusters, sector by sector.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Metadata Update</h4>
            <p>The directory entry and file record (inode/MFT entry) are updated with filename, timestamps, size, and
              cluster pointers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Journal Update</h4>
            <p>On journaled file systems (NTFS, ext4), the change is logged in the transaction journal before being
              committed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>File Deletion — The Forensic Treasure</h3>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-title">💡 Key Insight</div>
        Deleting a file does NOT erase the data. It only removes the pointer to the data. The actual bytes remain on
        disk until overwritten by new data — which may never happen.
      </div>

