      <h3>File Slack Space</h3>
      <p>
        When a file doesn't fill its last cluster completely, the remaining space is called <span class="highlight">file
          slack</span>. This slack space may contain fragments of previously stored files — a goldmine for forensic
        investigators.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, if a cluster is 4096 bytes and a file is 3000 bytes, the remaining 1096 bytes (called <strong>RAM
          slack</strong> + <strong>drive slack</strong>) may contain old data from previous files that occupied those
        sectors.
      </p>

      <h3>The Recycle Bin — More Than You Think</h3>
      <p>
        On Windows with NTFS, the Recycle Bin stores:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The deleted file itself (temporarily)</li>
        <li>An <code>$I</code> metadata file storing the <strong>original path</strong> and <strong>deletion
            timestamp</strong></li>
        <li>The actual file data in an <code>$R</code> file</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Even after emptying the Recycle Bin, the $I metadata may persist in unallocated space, revealing what was
        deleted and when.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 7 -->
    <section id="s7">
      <h2>7. Data Recovery Techniques</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>
      <p>Data recovery in forensics refers to extracting data from damaged, deleted, formatted, or corrupted storage
        media. There are several methods:</p>

      <h3>File Carving — The Signature Hunt</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="highlight">File carving</span> is the process of recovering files from unallocated space by
        searching for known <strong>file signatures</strong> (magic bytes) — without relying on file system metadata. If
        the directory entry is gone, carving finds the raw data by pattern matching.
      </p>

      <div class="callout info">
        <div class="callout-title">🔎 How File Carving Works</div>
        Every file type has a unique header (and often footer) called a "magic number." For example: JPEGs start with
        <code>FF D8 FF</code> and end with <code>FF D9</code>. Carving tools scan the raw image byte by byte looking for
        these patterns.
      </div>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>File Type</th>
            <th>Header (Hex)</th>
            <th>Footer (Hex)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>JPEG Image</td>
            <td><code>FF D8 FF</code></td>
            <td><code>FF D9</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PDF Document</td>
            <td><code>25 50 44 46</code> (%PDF)</td>
            <td><code>25 25 45 4F 46</code> (%%EOF)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PNG Image</td>
            <td><code>89 50 4E 47</code> (‰PNG)</td>
            <td><code>49 45 4E 44</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ZIP Archive</td>
            <td><code>50 4B 03 04</code> (PK..)</td>
            <td><code>50 4B 05 06</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>EXE/DLL</td>
            <td><code>4D 5A</code> (MZ)</td>
            <td>Variable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SQLite DB</td>
            <td><code>53 51 4C 69 74 65</code></td>
            <td>Variable</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Metadata Analysis</h3>
      <p>
        Even without file content, <strong>metadata</strong> tells a powerful story. Forensic examiners analyze:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>MAC Times:</strong> Modified, Accessed, Created — crucial for establishing timelines</li>
        <li><strong>EXIF Data:</strong> Embedded in photos — GPS coordinates, camera model, date taken</li>
        <li><strong>Document Properties:</strong> Author name, revision history, last saved time, company name</li>
        <li><strong>Thumbnail Cache:</strong> Windows stores thumbnails of images even after deletion</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Volume Shadow Copy (VSS) Analysis</h3>
      <p>
        Windows automatically creates <strong>Volume Shadow Copies</strong> — snapshots of the file system at different
        points in time. Forensic investigators use these to recover files that were deleted or modified after a shadow
        copy was taken, essentially "time traveling" through the drive's history.
      </p>

      <h3>SSD Forensics — The TRIM Challenge</h3>
      <p>
        Traditional recovery techniques struggle with SSDs because of the <span class="highlight">TRIM command</span>.
        When a file is deleted on an SSD with TRIM enabled, the OS tells the SSD controller to immediately zero out
        those blocks — making recovery nearly impossible. This is fundamentally different from HDDs.
      </p>
      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ SSD Forensics Warning</div>
        Always disable TRIM before forensic acquisition. On some modern systems, TRIM runs asynchronously in the
        background — connect SSD drives in write-blocked mode immediately upon seizure.
      </div>

      <h3>Partition Recovery</h3>
      <p>
        When a partition table is deleted or corrupted, the data may still be intact. Tools scan for file system
        superblocks (ext4), boot sectors (NTFS), or partition signatures to reconstruct the original layout.
      </p>

      <h3>RAID and Striped Volume Recovery</h3>
      <p>
        In enterprise environments, data is often spread across <strong>RAID arrays</strong>. Recovery requires
        understanding the RAID level, stripe size, and disk order:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>RAID 0:</strong> Striped — data split across disks, both needed for recovery</li>
        <li><strong>RAID 1:</strong> Mirrored — each disk is a full copy, easiest to recover</li>
        <li><strong>RAID 5:</strong> Striped with parity — can survive one disk failure</li>
        <li><strong>RAID 6:</strong> Dual parity — can survive two simultaneous disk failures</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

