      <h3>Legal Standards</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Daubert Standard (US):</strong> Scientific evidence must be testable, peer-reviewed, have known
          error rates, and be generally accepted</li>
        <li><strong>Frye Standard (some US states):</strong> Evidence must be generally accepted by relevant scientific
          community</li>
        <li><strong>ACPO Guidelines (UK):</strong> Four principles — no action should change data; if access necessary,
          competent person; audit trail must be created; person in charge has overall responsibility</li>
        <li><strong>ISO/IEC 27037:</strong> International standard for digital evidence handling</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Anti-Forensics — The Adversary's Toolkit</h3>
      <p>Suspects may try to defeat forensic analysis using:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Secure deletion tools:</strong> Eraser, DBAN, shred — overwrite data multiple passes</li>
        <li><strong>Encryption:</strong> BitLocker, VeraCrypt, FileVault — encrypted containers look like random data
        </li>
        <li><strong>Timestamp manipulation:</strong> Tools like Timestomp to alter MAC times</li>
        <li><strong>Steganography:</strong> Hiding data inside images or audio files</li>
        <li><strong>Living off the land:</strong> Using only built-in OS tools to avoid leaving traces</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 10 -->
    <section id="s10">
      <h2>10. Real-World Case Studies</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>

      <h3>Case Study 1 — The BTK Killer (2005)</h3>
      <p>
        Dennis Rader, known as the BTK (Bind, Torture, Kill) killer, evaded capture for 30 years. In 2005, he sent
        investigators a floppy disk believing it was untraceable. Forensic analysts recovered deleted metadata from the
        disk's unallocated space that contained <strong>the church name "Christ Lutheran"</strong> and a username —
        "Dennis." This led directly to his arrest and conviction. The deleted file's metadata, invisible to the naked
        eye, broke the case.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 2 — Operation Rescue (2010–2011)</h3>
      <p>
        International law enforcement agencies coordinated Operation Rescue, targeting a child exploitation network.
        Disk forensics across hundreds of seized computers revealed encrypted file containers. Using hash databases,
        investigators matched known illegal files even before decrypting them — hashing file content to compare against
        NCMEC's hash database without needing to view the content, protecting investigators while building airtight
        cases.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 3 — Enron Scandal (2001)</h3>
      <p>
        After Enron's collapse, investigators faced terabytes of email data. Forensic analysis of email servers
        recovered <strong>thousands of deleted emails</strong> from Exchange databases and backup tapes, despite Arthur
        Andersen (Enron's auditor) ordering document shredding. The recovered emails proved financial fraud and led to
        criminal charges against executives.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 4 — Ross Ulbricht / Silk Road (2013)</h3>
      <p>
        The FBI seized the laptop of Ross Ulbricht (founder of the dark web marketplace Silk Road) while he was actively
        logged in — a deliberate tactic to capture an unencrypted running session. Disk forensic analysis of the
        unencrypted drive revealed transaction logs, private keys, and chat histories that definitively linked him to
        the Silk Road operation, resulting in a life sentence.
      </p>

      <h3>Case Study 5 — Corporate Insider Theft</h3>
      <p>
        A major technology company suspected a departing engineer of stealing source code. Disk forensics on the
        employee's work laptop revealed: a large file copy to an encrypted USB drive 3 days before resignation
        (recovered from NTFS journal logs), evidence of <code>7-Zip</code> archives created and deleted (from thumbnail
        cache and LNK files), and browsing history showing competitor job applications. The employee was prosecuted
        under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act.
      </p>
    </section>

